<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <UserSecretsId>27cbe13d-36e2-4d53-bb6f-c0d704923fb7</UserSecretsId>
    <DockerDefaultTargetOS>Linux</DockerDefaultTargetOS>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      AutoMapper was dropped in favour of explicit mapping. 13.0.1 carries
      NU1903, a known high-severity advisory, and the two maps it held were
      `CreateMap<Activity, ActivityInfo>()` with no customisation — a dependency,
      a startup assembly scan and a security advisory to save writing out a
      dozen assignments that the compiler would otherwise have checked.
    -->
    <!--
      Ed25519, for the Runner handshake. .NET 8 has no Ed25519 — it arrives in
      the framework later — and this is the pure-managed option, so a container
      needs no native libsodium beside it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AWSSDK.S3" Version="4.0.102.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography" Version="2.7.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.OpenIdConnect" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!--
      The Data Protection key ring, in the database this installation already
      has. See `Authorization/KeyRing.cs` for why it is not left to the
      framework's default, and `docs/specs/AUTHENTICATION.md` §10 for why it is
      not Redis.

      **This adds nothing to the dependency closure.** Both halves of it —
      `Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection` and
      `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational` — are already pulled by the two
      references around it. Pinned to 8.0.0 to match them, so the packages that
      share a schema also share a version.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!--
      The LTI module validates a platform's `id_token` and signs its own client
      assertions with these. **Neither is a new dependency**: both were already
      in the closure, pulled by the OpenIdConnect handler above, and are promoted
      to direct references so the version on a signature-validation path is
      stated rather than inherited from a transitive graph that a minor bump
      elsewhere can move.

      **Never below what the handler resolves, and since 2026-08-29 above it by
      decision.** ASP.NET Core 10.0.11's OpenIdConnect asks for 8.19.2, and
      anything under that is an NU1605 downgrade — that is the floor. The
      ceiling used to be the same number, on the argument that raising it here
      raises it on the sign-in path as a side effect of an LTI feature. The
      owner overruled that when the dependencies were swept to their latest
      stable releases: **8.22.0 is deliberately three minors above the floor.**

      **What is covered is the LTI module, and not federated sign-in.**
      `FakePlatform` mints a genuinely signed `id_token` and `LaunchService`
      really validates it, so the library's own behaviour runs in the suite.
      The handler's copy does not: `FederatedSignInTests` deliberately begins
      after a validated principal exists, because state, nonce, PKCE and
      `id_token` validation are the framework's code rather than this product's.
      Signing in against a running deployment — `AlgoJudge-Identity-Keycloak`'s
      `scripts/e2e-against-server.py` — is what would exercise that half.

      MIT, maintained by Microsoft in `AzureAD/azure-activedirectory-identitymodel-extensions-for-dotnet`,
      shipping stable releases. No alpha or beta package goes on this path, which
      is a constraint the project owner set rather than a preference.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens" Version="8.22.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect" Version="8.22.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Tools" Version="10.0.11">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Azure.Containers.Tools.Targets" Version="1.23.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="Swashbuckle.AspNetCore" Version="10.2.3" />
    <!--
      YAML, for the pre-configuration an installation is stood up from. See
      `Preconfiguration/` and `docs/specs/PRECONFIGURATION.md` in the workspace.

      **The first third-party parser in this Server**, and worth saying out
      loud: everything else here reads JSON through the framework. It is used on
      one path — an operator's file on a mounted volume, read at first start or
      on `aj-admin config apply` — and never on a request body.

      YAML rather than TOML because the product already made this choice twice
      for hand-edited files: a package's `config.yml`, and the front matter of
      every statement. A third serialisation would be a third set of traps.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="YamlDotNet" Version="18.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Folder Include="Database\Migrations\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      A background worker's single unit of work is `internal` so nothing in the
      application calls it out of band, and visible to the tests so a sweep can
      be run against a clock somebody turns rather than waited for.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="AlgoJudge.Server.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
